\newcommand{\xmark}{\ding{55}}
\usepackage{placeins}

\newcommand{\Kstar}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Kempirical}{{K^*}}
\newcommand{\Ktheory}{{K^*_{\text{theory}}}}
\newcommand{\auc}{\text{AUC}}
\newcommand{\jsd}{\text{JSD}}
\newcommand{\tpm}{\ensuremath{\,\pm\,}}

\title{Generational Depth Estimation of Recursive Synthetic Text:\\Measuring the Discrimination Boundary}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% === Abstract ===
\begin{abstract}
Web-scale corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth.
We measure the discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$, defined as the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain statistically distinguishable, under prompt-chain generation using largely generator-invariant statistical features and an ordinal classifier.
The $\theta$-invariant domain ordering arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $=$ Code ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $>$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$) holds across six architecture families ($\leq$14B); within this hierarchy, $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ for web text emerges as the dominant mode (four of six families; changepoint-confirmed).
The domain ordering is feature-set invariant (preserved under both 15D statistics and DeBERTa fine-tuning), while absolute $\Kempirical$ values are feature-dependent: DeBERTa matches the 15D bound on C4 and Code but extends $\Kempirical$ to 3 on Wikipedia and 4 on arXiv, placing 15D measurements as conservative lower bounds.
Multi-source pooling preserves domain-calibrated $\Kempirical$ across all four domains with negligible generator-identity signal; features transfer across generators (80--92\% aggregate accuracy, driven by high 0v1 separability), but boundary-level $\Kempirical$ assignments fail under cross-model transfer (4/6 off-diagonal 7B pairs fall below $\theta$).
As a consistency check, $\Kempirical{=}2$ correctly predicts that binary filtering outperforms graduated alternatives for web text.
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

% === Section 2: Related Work ===
\input{related_work}

% === Section 3: Method ===
\input{method}

% === Section 4: Experiments ===
\input{experiments_c1}

% === Section 5: K*-Aware Data Filtering ===
\input{experiments_c3}

% === Section 6: Discussion ===
\input{discussion}

% === Section 7: Conclusion ===
\input{conclusion}

% === Limitations (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Limitations}

All experiments are conducted at $\leq$14B scale (6 architecture families, 10 configurations) with English text across four domains and four decoding strategies; whether $\Kempirical{=}2$ holds at 70B+ scales remains unverified.
Main results use prompt-chain only, isolating text-intrinsic degradation; retrain-chain yields $\Kempirical{=}7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}), and mixed-chain scenarios are not addressed.
Cross-model transfer retains 80--92\% aggregate accuracy but boundary-level $\Kempirical$ assignments remain fragile (4/6 off-diagonal 7B pairs fall below $\theta$; Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).

The 15D features yield a feature-set-specific lower bound; DeBERTa extends $\Kempirical$ by $+1$--$2$ on structured domains, suggesting headroom remains.
$\Kempirical$ is $\theta$-dependent (ranges from 2--3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$), though a $\theta$-free changepoint analysis agrees within $\pm 1$ for 35/37 configurations.
The filtering evaluation validates on two architectures with small effect sizes (max $+$1.92\,pp), demonstrating strategy ordering rather than large absolute gains.
Extended limitations appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

% === Ethics Statement (ACL required, unnumbered) ===
\section*{Ethics Statement}

Generational depth estimation is intended for data curation and corpus auditing, positive-sum applications that improve training data quality.
Our experiments use publicly available corpora (C4, Wikipedia, arXiv abstracts) and do not involve personally identifiable information.
A potential dual-use concern is that depth estimation techniques could inform adversarial strategies to evade detection (e.g., by calibrating rewriting to mimic earlier-generation statistics); we note that this risk is shared by all detection research and that the binary detection boundary ($\auc \geq 0.96$ at 0v1) remains robust.
Code and generated corpora will be released upon acceptance to enable reproducibility.

